#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13a7922-3441-5c68-ac31-ba6026553bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4\\ln(3)-3\\ln(8))}{\\ln(3)+2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264592} {:answer/id #uuid "de14078e-9826-53cb-871d-56c36cf00a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c13a7922-3441-5c68-ac31-ba6026553bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4\\ln(3)-3\\ln(8))}{\\ln(3)+2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6d66702-1876-50e0-935e-dff783b1570c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07e669d6-9649-5b20-abe3-70dd6ee4e891" :question/math-academy-id "q-242914" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${3}^{4-x}={8}^{2x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-x} & ={8}^{2x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{4-x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ -x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91695cd-31c2-5dd8-a3bc-8c8d39866e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(5\\ln(6)-\\ln(5))}{4\\ln(5)-7\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91695cd-31c2-5dd8-a3bc-8c8d39866e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(5\\ln(6)-\\ln(5))}{4\\ln(5)-7\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264596} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf7d9ab-8258-5d0c-b997-6f5651d1d1a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aea2ce2a-0f99-56fa-8c97-890df44863a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-242919" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{4x+1}={6}^{7x+5}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{4x+1} & ={6}^{7x+5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{7x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1cd8fd1-719a-53f2-8250-2dd6f52b9fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264599} {:answer/id #uuid "a7591086-c099-5a10-b96b-1e1751faf87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264600} {:answer/id #uuid "59c2d403-f4c5-512b-b9e4-67a2be33dcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264601} {:answer/id #uuid "315db262-e025-5508-a5b1-5528a6e23f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264602} {:answer/id #uuid "b349fc73-4e72-5c3d-b741-ad907e69d746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1cd8fd1-719a-53f2-8250-2dd6f52b9fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264599} :answer-field/id #uuid "de46083e-cd07-56ed-bc00-6d359161d91d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e7f287-adec-5973-9d8d-d6bf16f0de13" :question/math-academy-id "q-66792" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x}={2}^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & ={2}^{x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4bd7d5-9600-595e-855c-6bdd39428f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264606} {:answer/id #uuid "14051835-a242-5085-9536-8364699ab3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264607} {:answer/id #uuid "308d614f-9575-52fe-a980-5710414e17ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡3}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264608} {:answer/id #uuid "44820db2-c460-55d0-9512-4c4990ef2a84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264609} {:answer/id #uuid "5beb47bb-38f4-596c-be4d-beb3cf20ba2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14051835-a242-5085-9536-8364699ab3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264607} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d059f0-3026-5355-bdf0-ce4bfb21cd9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264605}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b29265f-c944-5a5b-bb00-edb60f6cc60f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66896" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-1}={3}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82ea3122-d5cb-5060-8d1c-7837073e95bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264613} {:answer/id #uuid "7916e07a-6421-5980-bca0-4fa59ae145e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264614} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd4eeeb-37d2-53df-8ce3-a4dda3f1d3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264615} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef409e6-42d4-54b9-ad50-fa666f95ff17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264616} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a8c3a9-3128-5e93-bcf9-c3216b719ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ea3122-d5cb-5060-8d1c-7837073e95bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264613} :answer-field/id #uuid "0246dc3c-86ad-59d8-8e3a-c92fbedcf356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad3aa12e-9200-54f0-8cef-9a72c4b36ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-66897" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-5}={4}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-5} & ={4}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & =5 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
